{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>From the stream</b> — The meaning is: the enemies were so afraid of him, because when they traveled on the roads he would drink from their streams and they had no power to stop him; therefore he would lift up the head. And some say: because of the multitude of his armies he needed to travel by a route where there was a stream, for springs would not suffice him, or the meaning is that waters brought to him on the roads are from flowing waters without fear, in the manner of 'give me water to drink' [Genesis 24:43]. And do not wonder at 'he shall drink' [being in the imperfect] and also 'he shall lift up' [being in the perfect], for behold 'he shall shatter' [is in the perfect while] 'the tongue' [is in the imperfect], and behold it concerns the way of the wilderness, at a standing time, and there are many like it."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 110, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra quotes Psalms 110:5–6 (מחץ ראש / לשון עבר) to illustrate the same mixing of perfect and imperfect forms in adjacent clauses; 'על זמן עומד' is rendered hyper-literally as 'at a standing time' since the phrase is elliptical and textually compressed."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:39:26.262400678Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}